+## 2026-10-07 — Phase 11b: Structured Logging, PII Scrubbing & Monitoring
+
+### D166. One in-repo structured logger for every process (`@ai-career/logging`), not pino
+**Decision:** `createLogger({ service })` writes one JSON line per event — `{ ts, level, service, event, ...fields }` — with `warn`/`error` to stderr and `debug`/`info` to stdout, filtered by `LOG_LEVEL` (default `info`). The logger's own four keys always win over fields. Level and redactor are process-wide (`configureLogging`, `defaultRedactor()`), read at write time, so loggers created at import time by library code (`packages/ai`'s `ai_usage_record_failed`, web's `response_model_failed`) follow the process's settings. The four workers' copied `log`/`safeErrorLabel` helpers and every production `console.*` call were replaced; event names are unchanged, the old `at` timestamp field is now `ts`, failures log at `error` and everything else at `info`. A structural test (`packages/logging/src/noConsole.test.ts`) fails on any `console.` call in production source.
+**Alternatives considered:** pino — its `redact` option removes fields by *path*; D9 scrubs by *value*, so the scrubber would be written anyway and pino would only add a dependency. Keeping the per-worker helpers — four copies of the same safety rule drift.
+**What it affects:** `packages/logging/src/{logger,sanitize,redactor,refresher,processLogging}.ts`, `services/*/src/main.ts`, `services/maintenance-worker/src/runOnce.ts`, `packages/ai/src/usage/track.ts`, `apps/web/src/lib/log.ts`, `apps/web/src/lib/insights/responseModel.ts`, `packages/config/src/env.ts`.
+
+### D167. Error messages are never logged; errors become `{ name, code?, causeName?, frames }`
+**Decision:** The sanitizer never reads an error's `message` (or its stack's first line). An error is logged as its class name, a `code` only when it is code-shaped (`^[A-Za-z0-9_:.-]{1,40}$` — SQLSTATE `23505`, `ECONNREFUSED`, our codes), the cause's name, and at most 8 stack frames as repo-relative `file:line:col` (`node:` and `node_modules` frames dropped; Next's bundle paths kept as `.next/...`). Strings are scrubbed then truncated at 500 characters; objects stop at depth 3, arrays at 50 items, cycles become `"[circular]"`. The one deliberate exception: the ingestion and matching workers' own `UnrecoverableError`/`IngestError`/`MatchingError` carry a fixed error class as their message, logged as `errorClass` as before.
+**Why:** Messages can echo resume, job or profile text that exact-value scrubbing cannot recognise (a Postgres unique-violation detail, a Zod issue, a provider error); architecture §9 already said "no resume/profile content in production logs regardless of scrubbing". Frames still say where a failure happened.
+**Accepted:** Debugging needs the frames plus reproducing the request; there is no switch to log messages.
+**What it affects:** `packages/logging/src/sanitize.ts`, the worker failure handlers.
+
+### D168. D9 implemented: exact profile values plus one email pattern, kept fresh per process
+**Decision:** `Redactor` replaces the user's `full_name`, `email`, `phone_number`, `address_line1` and `linkedin_url` (exact, case-insensitive, longest first, values under 3 characters ignored) with `[REDACTED]`, then any email address with `[REDACTED_EMAIL]`. `packages/db`'s `loadRedactionValues(db, userId)` reads them under RLS. Long-running processes call `initProcessLogging` at start (sets the level, loads the values before the first log line, refreshes every 60 s on an unref()'d timer); a failed load keeps the previous values and logs `redaction_refresh_failed` by error name. The web process loads lazily on the error path (`refreshWebRedactions`, at most once a minute, awaited before writing), so the first error after boot is still scrubbed.
+**Why the email pattern:** D9 rejected generic regexes for *names*, which have no reliable shape. Email addresses do, and recruiter/contact emails in application records are not the user's own values.
+**Accepted:** over-redaction (an address line that is also a common phrase is blanked everywhere in logs); under-redaction of name variants ("Jon" for "Jonathan"), as D9 accepted — both mitigated by never logging messages or content.
+**Note for tests:** the web test script loads the repo's `.env`, whose `DATABASE_URL` is the developer's own database. Route tests already mock `loadEnv` with the test database, so a refresh inside them reads the test database; the instrumentation test mocks the refresh outright. A `vi.mock` in the shared setup file was tried and does not apply to other test files here, so it was not used.
+**What it affects:** `packages/logging/src/{redactor,refresher,processLogging}.ts`, `packages/db/src/redactionValues.ts`, `apps/web/src/lib/webLogging.ts`, the worker entry points.
+
+### D169. Every API route is wrapped: an escaped error is a logged 500 with a request id, never Next.js's raw print
+**Decision:** All 40 `route.ts` files export `withRouteErrors("<route pattern>", handleX)`. A thrown error is logged as `request_failed` with `{ requestId, route, method, path (pathname only — the query string can carry search text), error }` and answered `500 { error: "Something went wrong (request <id>). Details are in the server log.", requestId }` with an `x-request-id` header; every AI panel already shows `body.error`. Handlers' own responses are untouched. `apps/web/src/lib/http/allRoutesWrapped.test.ts` fails if any route exports an unwrapped handler or a pattern that does not match its path. `instrumentation.ts`'s `onRequestError` logs `render_failed` for errors outside route handlers; because Next.js also compiles instrumentation for the Edge runtime, the logger is imported only inside the `NEXT_RUNTIME === "nodejs"` branch (Next's documented pattern) — a static import produced an Edge-bundle warning.
+**Why not `onRequestError` alone:** it only observes; Next.js still prints the raw error (message included) when an error escapes a handler.
+**Behaviour change:** two route tests that expected an error to escape (`/api/career-goal` with a goal missing its constraints, `/api/job-sources/upload` when storing fails) now assert the 500 body and that the internal detail does not leak.
+**Known gap:** for render errors Next.js still prints its own line; our pages are client components fetching wrapped routes, so this is rare.
+**What it affects:** `apps/web/src/lib/http/withRouteErrors.ts`, all `apps/web/src/app/api/**/route.ts`, `apps/web/src/instrumentation.ts`, `apps/web/src/lib/renderErrors.ts`.
+
+### D170. Worker heartbeats in Redis, and shutdown runs once
+**Decision:** `@ai-career/monitoring`'s `startHeartbeat(redis, worker)` writes `careerpilot:worker:<worker>` = `{ startedAt, beatAt, pid, stoppedAt }` at start and every 30 s (unref()'d timer, no TTL so "last seen 3 h ago" stays readable); `stop()` records a clean stop and is called in each worker's SIGINT/SIGTERM shutdown before Redis is closed. States: never_seen (no key), stopped (clean stop at/after the last beat), running (beat within `STATUS_STALE_AFTER_MS`, default 90 000, inclusive), stale (otherwise, or a malformed value). Workers: job-ingestion, matching, maintenance (optional), browser (optional). Write failures log `heartbeat_failed` by error name and never stop a worker. The entry points are not unit-testable, so `allWorkersBeat.test.ts` pins the wiring structurally and the E2E exercises it.
+**Found while wiring:** a second signal (a double Ctrl-C, or a wrapper such as dotenv-cli forwarding SIGTERM) re-entered the ingestion, matching and maintenance workers' `shutdown`; with the new awaited `heartbeat.stop()` the second run closed Redis under the first, crashing with an unhandled "Connection is closed". Those three now use the browser worker's existing `stopping` guard; the structural test requires it in all four.
+**Alternatives considered:** BullMQ's own worker metadata (`getWorkers()` lists connected clients but not "last seen" after a crash, and not workers that are not connected); a TTL'd key (a crashed worker would look identical to one never started).
+**What it affects:** `packages/monitoring/src/{workerState,heartbeat,status}.ts`, `services/*/src/main.ts`, `packages/config/src/env.ts`.
+
+### D171. `/api/status` and `/status`: always answer, never show a raw failure reason
+**Decision:** `GET /api/status` returns `{ checkedAt, database, redis, workers, queues }` and always 200; each check has a 5 s timeout and a down database or Redis is reported as `"unavailable"` (Redis down → no worker or queue status). Redis is opened fail-fast like the enqueue helpers, with `lazyConnect` and an explicit `connect()` first — with the offline queue disabled a `ping` sent before the socket is ready is rejected, which made a healthy Redis read as unavailable. Queue status is BullMQ's waiting/active/delayed/failed counts plus the newest failed job's time; its `failedReason` (BullMQ stores the raw `error.message`) is shown only when code-shaped (`^[a-z_]+(:[a-z0-9_]+)?$`), else "see the worker log". `/status` renders both tables, refreshes every 30 s and on "Refresh" with the newest response winning, notes that failed counts cover retained jobs only, and is linked from the home page as "9. System status — workers and queues" with no badge (a stopped optional worker is normal).
+**What it affects:** `apps/web/src/lib/status/{loadStatus,format}.ts`, `apps/web/src/app/api/status/route.ts`, `apps/web/src/app/status/`, `apps/web/src/app/page.tsx`.
+
 *Entries are appended chronologically. Do not edit or delete past entries when a decision is later reversed — add a new entry that supersedes it and cross-reference the original.*
